 Web consumers import core capabilities through `packages/core/index.ts`; storage stays in `packages/core/internal`. Core may use its own internals. Static import and re-export dependencies must be acyclic. These boundaries protect the approved separation of UI and storage; `boundaries.mjs` resolves TypeScript paths, including aliases, and rejects private web imports and cycles. No boundary exceptions are selected. Dynamic imports and runtime loading are outside this check and require review.
 
-`packages/web/public.ts` is the representative public-interface example: it imports `price` from `../core/index.ts` and exposes `quote()`. Quote remains 12. The controlled local HTTP endpoint in `journey.mjs` supplies 10; checkout must return `{ status: 201, total: 10 }` for the default quantity. `unit.test.mjs` protects quote and mocked checkout, while the journey observes a real loopback HTTP exchange. This fixture correction introduces no business pricing rule or discount.
+`packages/web/public.ts` is the representative public-interface example: it imports `price` from `../core/index.ts` and exposes `quote(quantity = 1)` for a selected quantity, multiplying that quantity by the public core price. Quote remains 12. The approved selected-quantity example is `quote(2) = 24`. The controlled local HTTP endpoint in `journey.mjs` supplies 10; checkout must return `{ status: 201, total: 10 }` for the default quantity. `unit.test.mjs` protects quote and mocked checkout, while the journey observes a real loopback HTTP exchange. This fixture correction introduces no business pricing rule or discount.
 
